# Append-only ledgers. Every one of them is written by MANY sessions at once -- measured
# 2026-08-24: 14 live worktrees of this repo across roughly 8 Claude sessions, and
# science/RESEARCH-LEDGER.jsonl has been touched by 15 commits. Two sessions each appending
# one row to the end of the same file is not a disagreement about anything, but git's default
# merge driver has no way to know that: it sees two different last lines and stops. That
# conflict happened for real today, on a file where both sides only ever added.
#
# merge=union keeps BOTH sides' lines instead of refusing. It is the correct driver for a file
# whose only operation is append, and the wrong one for a file where a line gets EDITED -- a
# union merge of an edit keeps both the old and the new line. So this list is exactly the
# append-only ledgers and nothing else. A file that starts being rewritten in place comes off
# this list the same day.
science/RESEARCH-LEDGER.jsonl merge=union
science/attention.jsonl       merge=union
science/predictions.jsonl     merge=union
science/ships.jsonl           merge=union
risk/REGISTER.jsonl           merge=union
#
# WHAT IT COSTS, stated so nobody discovers it later: a union merge appends the other side's
# lines to yours, so rows land in MERGE order and not in date order. Every row carries its own
# `date`, so anything that cares must sort rather than trust the file order. Proved both ways
# 2026-08-24 in a scratch repo: two branches each appending one row merged with both rows kept,
# and a file NOT on this list still conflicted on the same merge -- the driver is scoped, not
# blanket.
